<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.6.1: Checking Exponent Rules</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01333</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.6.1: Checking Exponent Rules</md:title>
    <md:uuid>a4d80e79-9faa-40f9-abe8-d47e15095601</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Warm Up (5 minutes)</title>
<para>This warm up prepares students to work with expressions involving negative exponents. Students have studied exponents
  and their properties in grade 8 and encountered negative exponents at that time. The goal here is to review the fact
  that for integer exponents \(m\) and \(n\) and a non-zero base \(b\), this property holds: \(b^m\cdot
  b^n=b^{m+n}\).</para>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<para>How would you rewrite each of the following as an equivalent expression with a single exponent? Use the '^' symbol to
  enter an exponent.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(2^4\cdot 2^0\)<emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(2^4\)
  </item><item> \(2^4\cdot 2^{-1}\)<emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(2^3\)
  </item><item> \(2^4\cdot 2^{-3}\)<emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(2^1\)
  </item><item> \(2^4\cdot 2^{-4}\)<emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(2^0\)
  </item>
</list>
<title>Activity Synthesis</title>
<para>Review, if needed, the conventions that \(2^0=1\) and \(2^{-1}=\frac12\), emphasizing that these conventions
  guarantee that \(2^a\cdot 2^b=2^{a+b}\) for any two integers \(a\) and \(b\).</para></section>
</content>
